        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/glencoe/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Alabama legislature dubbed Glencoe a “City Of Patriotism,” a title the town proudly embraces.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://cityofglencoe.org/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Every year, Glencoe hosts a Celebration in the Park featuring vendors, food, kids’ play, and an antique car and tractor show.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/glencoe/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Originally a farming community, Glencoe’s early economy relied on agriculture before industrial growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/glencoe/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town expanded after a rock quarry opened in 1853 and the Louisville & Nashville Railroad ran through it.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://encyclopediaofalabama.org/article/glencoe/"
    },
    {
